              `A sales interview is not like any other interview. Every other profession interviews you by assessing your knowledge and experience. Sales interviews assess that — and they also assess your sales skills in real time. The interviewer is watching how you handle pressure, how you listen, whether you ask good questions, how you respond to challenges. The meta-question behind every sales interview question is: "Would I trust this person to represent me in front of my customers?"`,
              `The STAR method turns vague answers into compelling evidence. Without it, most candidates answer competency questions with a rambling story that has no clear outcome. With it, your answers are structured, specific, and measurable. Always end with a Result that is quantified: "We increased meeting bookings by 40%" is better than "we improved our prospecting results."`,
              `Researching the company before an interview is table stakes — every candidate does it. What separates top candidates is asking better questions at the end of the interview. Most candidates ask safe questions: "What does a typical day look like?" or "What's the company culture like?" Elite candidates ask questions that demonstrate commercial thinking: "What's the biggest gap between where the sales team is now and where it needs to be in 12 months?" This question tells the interviewer you're already thinking like an AE.`
