              `Inbound is the best lead you will ever receive. The prospect has raised their hand — they've called, submitted a form, or walked into your store. They are already interested. The only way to fail with an inbound lead is to take that interest for granted and handle the conversation poorly. Yet most inbound call handling is amateurish: robotic scripted greetings, failure to understand what the customer specifically needs, and pressure-based tactics that undermine the natural buying energy the prospect arrived with.`,
              `The inbound sales conversation has a different opening than outbound. You don't need to earn attention — you already have it. Your first job is to understand what specifically prompted them to reach out, what they're hoping to get from the conversation, and what outcome they're looking for. These three pieces of information tell you how to frame the rest of the call.`,
              `One of the most powerful inbound techniques is the assumption of fit. When a customer calls about a product or service, you do not ask "do you want to proceed?" You guide the conversation assuming they will, and ask clarifying questions that move things forward: "Just so I can make sure we're pointing you at the right option — how many people will be using this?" This keeps the buyer in forward momentum without pressure.`
