              `Upselling and cross-selling are the most ethically loaded topics in B2C sales. Done wrong, they are exploitative: pressuring customers into things they don't need to hit an attachment target. Done right, they are a genuine service: helping customers avoid a frustrating gap in their purchase that they'll only notice after they've left.`,
              `The key distinction is timing and intent. An upsell offered before you fully understand what a customer needs is always a guess. An upsell offered after you know their situation and can see a genuine gap is a recommendation. The customer can feel the difference immediately. "Can I interest you in our extended warranty?" asked before any conversation is a target-driven add-on. "Based on how you've described using this laptop — taking it to different locations, heavy daily use — I'd strongly recommend the extended warranty rather than the basic cover" is a genuine recommendation.`,
              `The best upsell comes from a specific gap you've identified during the needs assessment. This is why discovery must precede any upsell offer. The question is not "would you like to add X?" The question is "I noticed during our conversation that [specific need] — have you considered how you'll handle that?" This reframes the additional product as a solution, not an addition.`
