              `The first email in a sequence should always be the most personalised and the shortest — under 75 words ideally. Each subsequent email should take a different angle: a different pain point, a different social proof, a different format (video message, question only, break-up email). The sequence should not feel like an escalating pursuit — it should feel like different, genuinely useful messages that a thoughtful person would send if they were trying to help.`,
              `The "break-up email" is typically the final email in any cold sequence. It explicitly acknowledges that you've reached out several times without a response, states that you won't contact them again after this, and makes one final low-pressure ask. Done correctly, break-up emails have the highest response rate of any step in the sequence — because they feel genuine (you're not bluffing about stopping) and because the prospect often feels a slight social obligation to respond.`
