              `What makes B2B SaaS selling different from other sales disciplines? First, the product is invisible — you cannot touch it, ship it, or hand it over in a box. The buyer is purchasing access to software and the value it creates. This makes storytelling and proof (case studies, demos, ROI models) far more important than they would be for physical products. Second, the buying decision is complex — it typically involves multiple stakeholders (IT, Finance, End Users, Executive sponsors), a detailed evaluation process, and a contract that ties the customer in for 1–3 years. Third, the relationship doesn't end at the signature — in SaaS, the renewal happens every year. A customer who doesn't get value will churn. A customer who gets exceptional value will expand. This makes discovery, implementation quality, and ongoing customer success directly tied to commercial outcomes.`,
              `The tech sales career is also unique in its scale of earning potential. An experienced AE at a Series B–D SaaS company carries a quota of £500K–£2M annually. At 100% attainment on a 15–20% commission rate, this means annual earnings of £75K–£400K. The learning curve is steep — the most successful tech AEs combine deep product knowledge, strong discovery skills, the ability to navigate complex buying committees, and a genuine interest in the technology space.`
