              `The same salesperson does not naturally excel in both SMB and enterprise selling. The skills and instincts developed in each environment are different — and sometimes opposite. An SMB AE who moves to enterprise brings great energy and urgency — but lacks the patience for long cycles and the discipline for deep stakeholder mapping. An enterprise AE who moves to SMB brings strategic thinking — but may be too slow, too formal, and too focused on perfecting the deal to hit the velocity required.`,
              `SMB selling requires speed and simplicity. The prospect makes decisions quickly, often alone or with one other person. A demo that lasts 45 minutes is too long. A proposal that runs 5 pages is too complex. The entire sales cycle — from first contact to signature — should be designed for speed: demos within a week of first contact, proposals sent the same day as the demo, follow-up within 24 hours. The AE who closes 20 SMB deals per quarter earns as much as the AE who closes 3 enterprise deals per quarter — but has a completely different workday.`,
              `Enterprise selling requires patience, precision, and political intelligence. The decision takes months. The buying committee is large. The legal and procurement process is slow and formal. The AE who gets frustrated by enterprise timelines or tries to rush the process creates resistance and loses deals. Enterprise success requires: champion development (building an internal advocate), stakeholder mapping (understanding every person in the buying committee), and executive access (building relationships at the C-suite level).`
