              `The selection of the first 10 customers matters as much as the acquisition of them. The wrong first 10 customers — the ones who are too large, too demanding, or in an industry that is not the eventual ICP — can derail the product roadmap, exhaust the team, and produce case studies that don't resonate with the intended market. The right first 10 customers are: a realistic size for the early product's capabilities, representative of the eventual target market, willing to provide honest feedback, and ideally willing to serve as references.`,
              `The commercial model for the first 10 customers is typically not the eventual commercial model. First customers often receive steep discounts, lifetime pricing guarantees, or additional services in exchange for being early. This is appropriate and strategic — the value of a happy first customer who becomes a reference and advocate is worth far more than the revenue difference between their pilot price and your eventual list price.`
