            raw: `**STEP 1 — COMPETITOR PRICING BENCHMARKS:**
What do the 3 closest alternatives charge? Position relative to them based on value differentiation.

**STEP 2 — VALUE-TO-PRICE CALCULATION:**
What is the measurable annual value you deliver to a typical customer? Set price at 10–20% of that value (the 10:1 to 5:1 ROI range that enterprise buyers expect).

**STEP 3 — WILLINGNESS TO PAY INTERVIEWS:**
In 5–10 sales conversations, ask: "What do you currently spend on this problem (tools, labour, workarounds)?" and "At what monthly cost would this start to feel like excellent value? At what point would it feel too expensive to justify?"

**STEP 4 — TIERED STRUCTURE:**
Create 3 tiers: Entry (individual/team use, limited features, lower price), Professional (team/department use, full features), Enterprise (company-wide, custom pricing).

**STEP 5 — TEST AND ITERATE:**
Price is not fixed — it should be tested and adjusted based on conversion rates, deal velocity, and customer feedback in the first 12 months.

💡 *Expert Insight:* Charge more than you're comfortable with. Then go higher. Most founders who have been through a pricing exercise end up concluding, 12 months later, that they priced too low when they launched. The customers you lose because of price are usually not your best customers. The customers who push back hard on price and still pay it are telling you something important: your product is worth it.`
