            raw: `**PRINCIPLE 1 — SIMPLICITY:**
A rep should be able to calculate their commission on a deal in their head. Max 3–4 components in any plan.

**PRINCIPLE 2 — ALIGNMENT WITH STRATEGIC PRIORITIES:**
What behaviours does the company need? Design the plan to reward them:
- Multi-year commitment needed → pay higher rate on 2+ year deals
- New product launch → SPIF on new product revenue
- Retention focus → clawback on early churn

**PRINCIPLE 3 — MOTIVATION ACROSS PERFORMANCE LEVELS:**
The plan should motivate median performers (achievable quota), top performers (accelerators above 100%), and identify underperformers (clear threshold below which performance management is appropriate).

**PRINCIPLE 4 — FAIRNESS:**
Equal opportunity (not identical territories, but equal expected revenue potential), transparent calculation (no black boxes), and predictable payout timing.

**EXAMPLE AE COMPENSATION STRUCTURE:**
- Base salary: £50,000
- OTE: £100,000 (50% base, 50% variable — standard for AE roles)
- Annual quota: £500,000 ARR
- Commission rate: 10% of ACV closed (up to 100% attainment)
- Accelerator: 15% of ACV on revenue above 100% quota
- Multi-year uplift: +2% on 2-year contracts, +3% on 3-year contracts
- Clawback: 100% of commission repayable if customer churns within 90 days

💡 *Expert Insight:* I have never seen a sales team that didn't figure out how to optimise their behaviour for the compensation plan within 60 days of it being published. This is not manipulation — it is rational human behaviour. If the plan rewards end-of-quarter discounting, the team will discount at end of quarter. If the plan rewards new logos more than expansion, the team will neglect expansion. Design the plan for the outcomes you actually want, not for the ones that are easiest to measure.`
