              `Executive conversations are fundamentally different from manager-level conversations. A VP of Operations wants to understand how the product works. A CFO wants to understand the financial return. A CEO wants to understand the strategic positioning implication — does this platform help us beat our competition, retain more customers, or enter a new market? The salesperson who delivers the same conversation to all three audiences wins the VP, loses the CFO, and confuses the CEO.`,
              `Executive presence is a learnable skill, but it requires deliberate development. The components: brevity (executives have limited time — the most important thing you can do is communicate clearly and concisely), substance (executives expect you to know their business, their market, and the competitive context), confidence (you are an expert advisor, not a vendor seeking approval), and listening (the most impressive thing you can do in an executive meeting is ask a question that shows you understand their business deeply).`
