              `Sales transformation — changing how a commercial organisation approaches customers, closes deals, and develops people — is one of the most challenging change management programmes available. It requires changing not just process and methodology, but habits, beliefs, and self-identity. An AE who has closed deals a certain way for 8 years will not change their behaviour in response to a methodology training day. Transformation requires: leadership role-modelling (the VP who doesn't use the new framework sends an unmistakable message), management reinforcement (every pipeline review should reference the new framework), and time (most methodology changes take 12–18 months to fully embed).`,
              `The most common transformation failure: launching a new methodology at an event, providing initial training, and then expecting it to be adopted through management pressure alone. Without structural reinforcement (CRM fields that reflect the new framework, coaching conversations anchored in the new methodology, compensation tied to framework adoption metrics, and ongoing training rather than a single event), behaviour reverts within 90 days.`,
              `Successful sales transformations are measured by outcomes — not activity. The question is not "did everyone attend the training?" but "did win rates improve? Did sales cycle length decrease? Did average deal size increase?" These outcome measures, tracked quarterly and compared to pre-transformation baselines, provide the evidence that the transformation is producing commercial return.`
